MCQS_CAT5 = r"""
    <!-- ═══════════ 5.2: Helm & Config (Q137–Q142) ═══════════ -->
    <div class="section-block" id="cat5-2"><h3>5.2 Helm &amp; Config (Q137–Q142)</h3>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q137" data-correct="B"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q137 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Helm chart repo for Cilium?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) https://helm.cilium.io/stable</div><div class="mcq-option">B) helm repo add cilium https://helm.cilium.io/ ; helm install cilium cilium/cilium --namespace kube-system</div><div class="mcq-option">C) helm install cilium stable/cilium</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Only via Cilium CLI, no Helm</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a137')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a137"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: B</div><p>Official Helm chart at https://helm.cilium.io/. Update: `helm repo update cilium`. Search versions: `helm search repo cilium/cilium --versions`.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q138" data-correct="C"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q138 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Helm values: kubeProxyReplacement options?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) true/false only</div><div class="mcq-option">B) enabled/disabled</div><div class="mcq-option">C) "disabled" (default), "partial" (ClusterIP only), "strict" (all services including NodePort). Strict needs: native devices, kernel 5.10+, eBPF Host Routing</div><div class="mcq-option">D) automatic detection only</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a138')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a138"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: C</div><p>Strict = full kube-proxy replacement with eBPF. Magic: `--set kubeProxyReplacement=strict`. NodePort, ExternalIPs, HostPort all via eBPF.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q139" data-correct="A"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q139 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">ipam.mode values and when to use each?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) kubernetes (default, uses podCIDR), cluster-pool (Cilium-managed CIDR pool, best for self-managed), eni (AWS), azure (Azure), multi-pool (multiple CIDRs for multi-tenant)</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Only kubernetes and cluster-pool</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Only cloud-specific modes</div><div class="mcq-option">D) dhcp mode for all environments</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a139')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a139"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: A</div><p>cluster-pool: `--set ipam.mode=cluster-pool --set ipam.operator.clusterPoolIPv4PodCIDRList=10.0.0.0/8`. Good for bare metal. eni/azure: cloud-native IPAM.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q140" data-correct="D"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q140 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">routingMode and tunnel settings?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Always use VXLAN</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Always use native routing</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Only Geneve is supported</div><div class="mcq-option">D) tunnel: "vxlan" (default, overlay), "geneve", "disabled" (native routing, needs router BGP/static routes). routingMode: "native" (direct L3), "tunnel" (encapsulation). Auto-detected per cloud</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a140')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a140"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: D</div><p>Native: pods directly routable, better performance, needs route distribution. Tunnel: works anywhere, slight overhead. Cloud defaults: EKS=eni, GKE=geneve, AKS=azure.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q141" data-correct="B"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q141 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">encryption.enabled + encryption.type values?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Only WireGuard</div><div class="mcq-option">B) encryption.enabled=true + encryption.type="wireguard" (kernel 5.6+) OR "ipsec" (any kernel, more CPU). WireGuard: simpler, faster. IPSec: broader kernel support, more complex config</div><div class="mcq-option">C) TLS-based encryption only</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Encryption not supported in Cilium</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a141')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a141"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: B</div><p>WireGuard: kernel 5.6+, port 51871, simpler key management. IPSec: any kernel, uses XFRM, more CPU overhead. Both transparent to pods.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q142" data-correct="C"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q142 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Helm --set vs --values for production?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) --set only — simpler</div><div class="mcq-option">B) No difference</div><div class="mcq-option">C) --values values.yaml for production (GitOps, version controlled, reproducible). --set for quick overrides/dev. Best: ArgoCD/Flux with values.yaml in Git</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Production uses CLI only</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a142')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a142"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: C</div><p>GitOps pattern: store values.yaml in Git, ArgoCD syncs. Never `--set` in production — no audit trail, not reproducible. One values.yaml for each cluster.</p></div></div>
    </div>

    <!-- ═══════════ 5.3: Upgrades & Ops (Q143–Q148) ═══════════ -->
    <div class="section-block" id="cat5-3"><h3>5.3 Upgrades &amp; Ops (Q143–Q148)</h3>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q143" data-correct="A"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q143 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Pre-upgrade checks for Cilium?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Read upgrade notes (BREAKING CHANGES), check CRD schema required updates, verify Helm values deprecated keys, run cilium connectivity test BEFORE, check kernel compatibility with new version, backup etcd/CRDs</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Just run cilium upgrade</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Only check kernel version</div><div class="mcq-option">D) No checks needed — seamless</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a143')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a143"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: A</div><p>ALWAYS read upgrade notes first. Breaking changes between minors. CRD schema changes require `kubectl apply -f` before Helm upgrade. Run connectivity test pre-upgrade for baseline.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q144" data-correct="B"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q144 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Rolling back a Cilium upgrade?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Impossible — must reinstall cluster</div><div class="mcq-option">B) `helm rollback cilium` — but eBPF programs may be NEWER than agent. If CRD schema changed, must also revert CRDs. Test rollback in staging first</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Just reinstall old version</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Cilium auto-rollback on failure</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a144')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a144"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: B</div><p>Key risk: eBPF programs loaded by new agent may not be understood by old agent. Solution: restart pods to reload eBPF programs. Always test rollback in staging.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q145" data-correct="C"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q145 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Cilium agent DaemonSet upgrade strategy?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Recreate — all pods restart at once</div><div class="mcq-option">B) OnDelete only</div><div class="mcq-option">C) RollingUpdate (default). eBPF programs persist in kernel during restart — connections survive. Max unavailable: 2 or 10%. Node-by-node, 30s wait between nodes</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Blue/Green deployment</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a145')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a145"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: C</div><p>DaemonSet uses RollingUpdate. Key: eBPF programs stay loaded in kernel while agent restarts. Existing connections survive. New connections use new agent.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q146" data-correct="A"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q146 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">cilium config vs cilium-config ConfigMap?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) `cilium config` reads/writes the cilium-config ConfigMap in kube-system. Runtime changes via `cilium config set Key=Value`. Persistent across agent restarts</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Two separate config systems</div><div class="mcq-option">C) ConfigMap is deprecated — use CLI only</div><div class="mcq-option">D) CLI config is local only, not cluster-wide</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a146')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a146"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: A</div><p>`cilium config` is a convenience wrapper around the ConfigMap. Changes propagate to all agents. `cilium config view` shows current runtime config.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q147" data-correct="D"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q147 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Uninstall Cilium cleanly?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) kubectl delete ns kube-system</div><div class="mcq-option">B) helm uninstall cilium — done</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Just delete the DaemonSet</div><div class="mcq-option">D) `cilium uninstall` OR `helm uninstall cilium -n kube-system`. Then: remove CNI config from /etc/cni/net.d/, reboot nodes or remove veth/bpffs manually. Install new CNI before pods break</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a147')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a147"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: D</div><p>cilium uninstall cleans up Cilium resources. After uninstall: CNI config still on nodes. Remove /etc/cni/net.d/05-cilium.conf. bpffs cleanup on reboot.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q148" data-correct="B"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d5">Installation</span> Q148 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Cilium in air-gapped / offline environment?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Not supported</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Pre-pull images to private registry. `cilium install --set image.repository=my-registry/cilium/cilium --set operator.image.repository=...`. Use --version for specific tag. Helm chart must be available locally</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Cilium auto-detects offline mode</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Only via manual DaemonSet YAML</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a148')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a148"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: B</div><p>Key images: cilium-agent, operator, hubble-relay, hubble-ui, clustermesh-apiserver, preflight. Mirror all to internal registry. Helm chart: download tgz or use local path.</p></div></div>
    </div>
"""

