MCQS_CAT7 = r"""
    <!-- ═══════════ 7.x: Advanced eBPF (Q177–Q188) ═══════════ -->
    <div class="section-block" id="cat7-adv"><h3>Advanced eBPF in Cilium (Q177–Q188)</h3>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q177" data-correct="B"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q177 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">eBPF verifier — what does it check?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Only syntax</div><div class="mcq-option">B) No unbounded loops (must terminate), all pointer accesses checked (null + bounds), no unreachable instructions, max 1M instructions (5.1+), stack size limit (512 bytes), helper function arguments validated. Any violation → program rejected</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Only memory safety</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Runtime behavior monitoring</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a177')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a177"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: B</div><p>The verifier is eBPF's safety guarantee — proves the program cannot crash the kernel. All loops must be provably bounded. Complex programs may need `#pragma unroll`.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q178" data-correct="C"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q178 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">BPF CO-RE (Compile Once, Run Everywhere)?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Run same binary on any OS</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Cross-architecture compilation</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Single eBPF binary works on different kernel versions via BTF (BPF Type Format). Kernel provides type info at load time. Relocations resolve field offsets. No need to compile per kernel</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Container-optimized eBPF</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a178')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a178"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: C</div><p>Before CO-RE: compile eBPF for each kernel version (like kernel modules). With CO-RE + BTF: one binary, kernel BTF provides struct layouts. Kernel 5.4+ with BTF enabled. Cilium uses CO-RE extensively.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q179" data-correct="A"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q179 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">BPF maps in Cilium — types and purposes?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) HASH: policy lookup, identity map. ARRAY: per-endpoint config. LRU_HASH: connection tracking (conntrack). LPM_TRIE: CIDR routing. PERCPU_HASH: per-CPU counters. SOCKMAP: socket redirection for L7 proxy</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Only HASH maps</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Only ARRAY maps</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Cilium doesn't use BPF maps</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a179')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a179"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: A</div><p>Each map type optimized for specific use: HASH for O(1) policy lookup, LPM for longest-prefix CIDR match, LRU for automatic eviction in conntrack. Inspect: `bpftool map dump pinned /sys/fs/bpf/tc/globals/cilium_ct4_global`.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q180" data-correct="B"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q180 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">eBPF hook points used by Cilium?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Only XDP</div><div class="mcq-option">B) TC (traffic control) ingress/egress — main policy + LB. XDP (eXpress Data Path) — early DDoS/ACL. Socket — L7 proxy redirection. cgroup — socket-level policy. kprobe/kretprobe — minimal use. Cilium primarily uses TC hooks</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Only cgroup hooks</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Netfilter hooks (same as iptables)</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a180')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a180"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: B</div><p>TC is Cilium's workhorse — attaches to veth host side for ingress+egress. XDP for early drop (optional). Socket for Envoy proxy bypass. cgroup for socket-level enforcement.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q181" data-correct="D"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q181 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Maglev load balancing in eBPF?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Traditional round-robin in eBPF</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Random backend selection</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Least-connections algorithm</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Consistent hashing algorithm implemented in eBPF. Backend table with fixed size M (typically 65537). Each backend hashes to ~M/N slots. Backend changes only affect ~1/N of flows. O(1) lookup</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a181')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a181"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: D</div><p>Maglev provides consistent hashing for service backends. When backend is added/removed, only connections mapped to that backend shift — rest unchanged. Critical for connection tracking stability.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q182" data-correct="A"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q182 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">eBPF tail calls in Cilium?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Chain multiple eBPF programs without returning to kernel. Used for complex policy chains: TC ingress → tail call to policy program → tail call to LB → tail call to forward. Avoids stack overflow, modular design</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Recursive function calls</div><div class="mcq-option">C) System calls from eBPF</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Calling user-space programs</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a182')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a182"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: A</div><p>Tail calls = eBPF's "function call." Max 33 tail calls deep. Cilium uses them for modular policy processing: DNAT → policy → LB → forward. `bpftool prog show` reveals the chain.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q183" data-correct="C"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q183 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">eBPF bandwidth management — EDT (Earliest Departure Time)?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Token bucket filter in kernel</div><div class="mcq-option">B) HTB qdisc</div><div class="mcq-option">C) eBPF implements EDT rate limiting: each packet gets assigned a departure timestamp. If "now < departure time," packet is queued. Provides precise bandwidth control per-pod without qdisc overhead</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Uses iptables rate limiting</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a183')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a183"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: C</div><p>EDT in eBPF replaces tc-tbf/HTB. More precise, less CPU. Set via CiliumNetworkPolicy: `egress: {bandwidth: "10Mbps"}`. Kernel 5.18+ for BBR + EDT.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q184" data-correct="B"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q184 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">bpffs (BPF File System) purpose?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Temporary eBPF storage</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Pseudo-filesystem at /sys/fs/bpf/ for PINNING eBPF programs and maps. Pinned objects persist beyond process lifetime. Cilium pins maps at /sys/fs/bpf/tc/globals/cilium_*. Enables sharing between agent and CLI tools</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Logging for eBPF programs</div><div class="mcq-option">D) eBPF source code storage</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a184')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a184"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: B</div><p>Without bpffs: eBPF objects die when the loading process exits. With bpffs: maps survive agent restarts. `ls /sys/fs/bpf/tc/globals/` shows Cilium's pinned objects. `bpftool map show pinned /sys/fs/bpf/...`.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q185" data-correct="A"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q185 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">XDP vs TC — when does Cilium use each?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) TC is primary (all features). XDP is OPTIONAL for early packet drop (DDoS mitigation, ACL). XDP runs BEFORE kernel allocates sk_buff — faster for drop. But XDP can't do L7, LB, conntrack. Cilium uses TC for everything by default</div><div class="mcq-option">B) XDP replaces TC completely</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Both used equally</div><div class="mcq-option">D) XDP for ingress, TC for egress only</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a185')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a185"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: A</div><p>XDP advantage: runs before SKB allocation — 10M+ pps drop capability. Limitation: no stateful processing, no L7. Cilium uses TC 99% of the time. Enable XDP with `--set bpf.acceleration=native`.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q186" data-correct="C"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q186 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">eBPF program limit per node?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Unlimited</div><div class="mcq-option">B) 10 programs per node</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Cilium loads ~10-20 eBPF programs per endpoint (pod). With 500 pods = 10,000+ programs. Kernel limit: typically 1M total instructions. Practical: depends on kernel memory. Cilium scales to thousands of pods</div><div class="mcq-option">D) 1 program per node</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a186')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a186"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: C</div><p>Each Cilium endpoint (pod) gets TC ingress + egress programs. Kernel handles many programs efficiently. `bpftool prog list | wc -l` to count. `bpftool prog show` for details.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q187" data-correct="B"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q187 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">eBPF ring buffer vs perf buffer for observability?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) Same — different name</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Ring buffer (newer, kernel 5.8+): shared memory, lockless, higher throughput, variable-size records. Perf buffer (older): per-CPU, fixed-size, more CPU overhead. Hubble uses ring buffer for flow events</div><div class="mcq-option">C) Perf buffer is newer</div><div class="mcq-option">D) Hubble doesn't use either</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a187')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a187"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: B</div><p>Ring buffer advantage: no per-CPU ordering issues, better batching, lower overhead. Hubble exports flows from kernel → user space via ring buffer. Kernel 5.8+ recommended.</p></div></div>

    <div class="mcq-block" id="q188" data-correct="A"><div class="mcq-number"><span class="domain-pill d7">eBPF</span> Q188 of 200</div><div class="mcq-question">Cilium eBPF datapath debugging?</div><div class="mcq-options"><div class="mcq-option">A) cilium bpf endpoint list, cilium bpf policy get <ep-id>, cilium bpf ct list, cilium bpf lb list, cilium map list. bpftool for kernel-level: bpftool prog show, bpftool map dump. cilium monitor for live events</div><div class="mcq-option">B) Only cilium monitor</div><div class="mcq-option">C) tcpdump on host interfaces</div><div class="mcq-option">D) No debugging tools — black box</div></div><button class="mcq-toggle-btn" onclick="toggleAnswer('a188')">👁️ Show Answer &amp; Explanation</button><div class="mcq-answer" id="a188"><div class="answer-label">✅ Correct Answer: A</div><p>Debugging layers: cilium CLI (high-level) → bpftool (kernel-level) → cilium monitor (live events). `cilium bpf endpoint list` shows all local endpoints with their BPF program IDs.</p></div></div>
    </div>
"""

